The bundle you’re describing has several existing names, but no single one captures all of it. The closest clean term is capability masking under strategic latency: an agent possesses capability, deliberately under-signals it, observes, accumulates information, and preserves optionality until conditions change.

Related pieces are feigned weakness, strategic opacity, deceptive quiescence, low-signature posture, and—when the point is to induce a false model in an observer—adversarial signaling. “Smurfing” captures only the competence-understatement piece.

The punch/kiss part is a different but connected phenomenon: a salient state-transition event. One sufficiently strong event changes the observer’s model, and then ordinary later behavior is interpreted through a new prior. In Bayesian language, the event causes a large posterior update. In dynamical-systems language, it can create hysteresis: after the system crosses a threshold, returning the input to its old level does not immediately restore the old state.

So:

ordinary behavior + old prior -> interpretation A

but after a salient event:

same ordinary behavior + updated prior -> interpretation B

That is why one unmistakable demonstration of strength, tenderness, danger, loyalty, competence, betrayal, or affection can propagate far beyond the original event. The event is local; the model update is global.

Your “different elasticities” language also maps well. Different layers have different response functions:

input -> layer-specific gain -> state change -> downstream propagation

A physical signal, emotional signal, social signal, narrative signal, and institutional signal can all receive the same event differently. Some layers absorb it. Some amplify it. Some retain it for years.

For Pantheon language, I’d compress the whole thing into three operators:

Latent Capability — capability exists without continuous display.

Threshold Demonstration — one sufficiently salient event proves a hidden variable strongly enough to update the observer’s model.

Persistent Reframing / Hysteresis — subsequent signals are interpreted through that new model even after the original event is gone.

That is stronger and more precise than “pretending to be dumb.” The interesting object is not the disguise itself. It is the separation between internal state, emitted signal, inferred state, and the event that suddenly collapses uncertainty between them.